WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:23.590
He was the undisputed king of rock n’ roll.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.970
In the mid-1950s, 20-year old Elvis Presley
exploded onto the airwaves.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:32.820
With a rockabilly blend of country music and
blues, his curled lip, and his trademark wiggling

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.550
hips, the southern boy caused a sensation.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:40.940
Coming just as youth culture was taking off,
Elvis was everything these new teenagers wanted:

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.800
dangerous, rebellious, and anathema to their
parents.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.860
Across the nation, preachers denounced him
from their pulpits.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.079
Judges issued writs against him for corrupting
morals.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:54.500
The National Guard was even called to his
concerts.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:01:00.920
If you were alive in 1956, then you likely
thought Elvis either the sexiest or most-dangerous

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.330
man alive.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.360
Yet who feels that way today?

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.740
Mention Elvis’s name now, and you’re more
likely to conjure images of a fat, sweaty

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:14.759
dude warbling away in Las Vegas, his preposterous
white suit still stained with the grease of

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:17.520
ten thousand hamburgers.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.340
How did the King of rock n’ roll transform
into its blubbery punchline?

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:29.149
Today, Biographics is taking its Delorean
all the way back to the 1950s to witness the

00:01:29.149 --> 00:01:35.460
birth of a generation… and the life of that
generation’s greatest icon.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:41.090
(TITLE): Livin’ in a Shotgun Shack

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.119
Although he grew up to become the King, there
was nothing noble about the story of Elvis’s

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:46.119
birth.

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:50.420
On January 8, 1935, Elvis Aaron Presley was
born into a world that was groaning under

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:51.659
the weight of the Great Depression.

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:56.409
His father, Vernon Presley, was an odd-job
man, while his mother, Gladys, was barely

00:01:56.409 --> 00:01:57.509
scraping by.

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:01.700
The two lived in East Tupelo, a small town
known back then as one of the roughest places

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:02.700
in Mississippi.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.630
Although the family did have their own house
- a two room shotgun shack practically small

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:11.800
enough to fit inside your average briefcase
- it was without electricity, indoor plumbing,

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.069
or much of anything else.

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:17.670
In fact, the Presleys were so poor that they
had to rely on welfare to pay the doctor who

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.709
delivered Elvis.

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:22.120
Or rather, delivered Elvis and his twin.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.230
Yep, twin.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:27.500
Elvis was originally meant to be brother to
Jesse Garon Presley, but the younger twin

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:28.830
emerged stillborn.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.860
He almost wasn’t the early one.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:35.400
The birth nearly killed both Gladys and Elvis,
and the two were rushed to hospital.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.430
Although both pulled through, the shock of
losing Jesse seems to have permanently scarred

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:40.430
Gladys.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.260
For the rest of her life, she would be ferociously
overprotective of Elvis.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.070
But that was all for the future.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:51.130
For the first part of his life, baby Elvis
experienced nothing but nonstop deprivation.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:55.520
Gladys and Vernon were so poor that they had
to rely on their neighbors to buy diapers.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.030
Shockingly, this was their financial highpoint.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:04.310
In 1938, Vernon was jailed for forging a $4
check, and their shack was repossessed.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.080
As Gladys moved about, staying with friends,
three year old Elvis took to sitting on the

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.430
front steps, bawling his eyes out for his
dad to come home.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:17.410
Yet, even after Vernon’s early 1939 release
for good behavior, things still didn’t improve

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:18.410
for the family.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:22.120
They spent years bouncing between different
rental places, moving in and out of public

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.120
assistance housing.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.830
There’s a great little legend attached to
this.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.950
Aged four, Elvis is supposed to have overheard
his parents arguing about money, and told

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:33.910
them not to worry, because he’d buy them
a cadillac one day for sure.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:38.780
Needless to say, this childish boast would
turn out to be more accurate than Vernon or

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.490
Gladys could’ve ever predicted.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:41.720
And so life passed.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.800
Vernon continued doing odd-jobs, the family
continued wallowing in poverty, and Elvis

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.410
finally started school.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:52.140
But, even now, there were signs of the legend
he would become.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.620
In 1945, one of Elvis’s teachers overheard
him singing.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.900
She was so impressed by his voice that she
took him to the principal, who was so impressed

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.400
he insisted Elvis enter a talent show.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.110
That was how, on October 3, ten year old Elvis
came to stand up at the Mississippi-Alabama

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.030
Fair and Dairy Show and sing “Old Shep.”

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:15.640
The event was broadcast on WELO Radio, the
first time the world - or Mississippi, at

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.130
any rate - heard Elvis Presley’s voice.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:21.989
While we’d love to tell you that some stock
character in the studio immediately cried

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:23.130
“gadzooks!

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:28.530
This kid’s got something!”, the reality
was that nothing so narratively pleasing happened.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.880
Instead, Elvis placed a paltry fifth in the
talent show, and that was that.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.539
Except, of course, it wasn’t.

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:37.840
On Elvis’s 11th birthday, Gladys took him
to buy a present.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.950
Elvis was determined to either get a rifle
or a bike, but both gifts were so dangerous

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:47.840
that just thinking about them gave the overprotective
Gladys palpitations.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.510
So she convinced her son to make do with a
cheaper gift instead, one which would keep

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:53.640
him out of trouble.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.790
And that’s how, in January, 1946, Elvis
Presley got his first guitar.

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:00.400
(TITLE): Home of the Blues

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:08.880
The next big shift in young Elvis’s life
came in November, 1948.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.550
That month, Vernon and Gladys moved all the
way north to Memphis, hoping for a fresh start.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:18.410
For their music-loving son, it was like spending
your entire life listening to only one tune,

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:24.370
only to suddenly discover Spotify exists and,
dear God, music is everywhere!

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:29.540
Although the family were still in public housing,
it was now in a city thrumming with blues,

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:34.150
with country, with gospel, with anything that
might make you dance.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.570
At school, too, the new city meant a new Elvis.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:41.580
Suddenly, the boy was wearing his hair long
- well, long for the fifties - and slicked

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:45.350
back, and pairing it with colorful, stylish
outfits.

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:48.900
Out went Elvis, the momma’s boy and teacher’s
pet.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:51.200
In came Elvis: the rock n’ roll rebel.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.000
At least, that was the image.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.780
In reality, Elvis pretty much stayed out of
trouble, despite what his swagger might’ve

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:58.780
suggested.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:04.010
OK, so let’s jump forward a few years now;
past the end of the forties, past the Korean

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:10.700
War, and right on to the summer of 1953, where
Elvis has just graduated and got a job driving

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:11.700
trucks.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:17.770
At this point, America is on the cusp of becoming
what you imagine when you think “1950s USA”.

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:21.210
The portable transistor radio was months away
from debuting.

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:26.139
Little Richard had just signed with Peacock
Records, and rock n’ roll was slowly becoming

00:06:26.139 --> 00:06:27.490
a Thing.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:32.040
Teens were starting to become consumers, leading
to the first ripples of youth culture.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.830
In short, it was a world transforming, with
a new, teen-led culture just waiting for its

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:37.830
leader.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.310
It would find him in Memphis.

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:44.520
That summer, 1953, Elvis got a $4 advance
from his job and went to the Memphis Recording

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.270
Service to cut a single.

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:50.199
In later years, the King would say he simply
wanted to hear what he sounded like.

00:06:50.199 --> 00:06:55.610
But since the service was owned by Sun Records’
Sam Phillips, it seems more likely that Elvis

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:57.170
wanted to get noticed.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:59.320
Not that that immediately happened.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:03.390
After famously telling the assistant at the
service “I don’t sound like nobody,”

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:06.350
Elvis cut his record, paid his four bucks…

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:08.330
...and nothing.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:12.540
For a whole year Elvis waited, driving trucks
and hoping for the best.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:17.729
At some point in those twelve months, he must’ve
started to wonder if his music career was

00:07:17.729 --> 00:07:19.240
gonna go anywhere.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:23.440
Then, on June 26, 1954, he finally got the
call.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.320
Marion Keisker at Sun Records wanted to know
if he could be at the studio by three.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.701
As Elvis later said: “I was there by the
time she hung up the phone.”

00:07:32.701 --> 00:07:37.300
The session Elvis sang in that day wasn’t
great, but it was good enough for Sam Phillips

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.540
to call him back on July 5.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:42.100
That day, nothing much seemed to be happening
in the booth.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:43.280
No magic, no nothing.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:45.050
Bummed out, everyone took a break.

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:46.449
And that’s when it happened.

00:07:46.449 --> 00:07:50.889
To unwind, Elvis casually started playing
a version of Arthur Crudup’s blues song

00:07:50.889 --> 00:07:54.669
That’s All Right, only with a faster tempo
and a laid back style.

00:07:54.669 --> 00:07:58.479
As he played, session guitarist Scotty Moore
and bassist Bill Black joined in.

00:07:58.479 --> 00:08:02.570
Suddenly, the song was transformed into something
more than blues.

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:04.389
Into a whole new sound.

00:08:04.389 --> 00:08:06.979
The trio cut their single that same day.

00:08:06.979 --> 00:08:10.949
None of them could’ve known it as they left
the studio and stepped out into the warm Memphis

00:08:10.949 --> 00:08:14.389
evening, but they’d just found their golden
ticket.

00:08:14.389 --> 00:08:18.810
When That’s All Right hit the airwaves,
it was gonna transform their lives.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:24.870
(TITLE): That was the Summer of ‘54…

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.690
When fame came for Elvis, it didn’t slowly
court him.

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:33.640
It grabbed him like some kind of out-of-control
fame hawk, dragging him straight up into the

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:34.640
stratosphere.

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:40.380
On July 8, local station WHBQ was handed Elvis’s
record by Sam Phillips and asked to play it.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:43.690
When they did, the switchboard lit up like
a Christmas tree.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:47.769
So many people called in, that WHBQ played
That’s All Right again.

00:08:47.769 --> 00:08:49.029
And again.

00:08:49.029 --> 00:08:50.290
And again.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:54.670
By the time the show ended, Elvis’s record
had been heard all over the state.

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:55.990
People were going nuts.

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:58.620
From that point on, Elvis’s destiny was
fixed.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:03.320
He was gonna be the biggest damn star Memphis
had ever seen.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:06.410
That summer was a wild one for the boy and
his musicians.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:10.839
From driving trucks, Elvis was suddenly being
driven all over Tennessee, all over the Deep

00:09:10.839 --> 00:09:13.070
South, playing endless gigs.

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:16.120
Because they only had that one record, that’s
all they played.

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:20.620
Johnny Cash later recalled seeing Elvis at
one gig just sing That’s All Right over

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:23.400
and over, like a record stuck on repeat.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:24.720
Yet people loved it.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.520
Try and image, for a moment, how it must’ve
felt to be Elvis Presley that summer.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:33.200
How it must’ve felt, driving from town to
town, the windows rolled down and a cool breeze

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:37.180
blowing through your slicked-back hair, knowing
that your time had come.

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:38.560
It must’ve been magical.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.710
The sort of feeling you can’t ever recapture.

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:46.870
On October 16, Elvis played the Louisiana
Hayride radio show, his voice transmitting

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:48.350
to 13 states.

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:52.660
The response was so good that the producers
had him sign a year-long contract then and

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:53.660
there.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:55.700
It meant enough money to quit his day job.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:59.270
But Hayride is important in the Elvis story
for another reason.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:03.870
A month after Elvis began his contract, a
shady music promoter began working with Hayride.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:08.870
A guy who claimed he came from West Virginia,
but couldn’t quite hide his Dutch accent.

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:12.149
Yep, it’s time to introduce Colonel Tom
Parker.

00:10:12.149 --> 00:10:17.050
A major impresario in the south, Parker is
a guy with a background so strange it’s

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:20.000
tempting to think he stepped out a Hollywood
noir.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.940
He claimed to be American, but was really
from the Netherlands.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:27.920
His title of colonel was only honorary, and
the one time he’d really been in the Army

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:29.220
he’d deserted.

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:33.620
His past was full of weird tales, like running
carnival roadshows or intentionally eating

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:36.270
until he was too fat to fight in WWII.

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:40.270
He likely wasn’t a naturalized US citizen;
and it’s even thought today he may have

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:42.190
fled the Netherlands after killing someone.

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:45.210
But Parker did have one vital skill.

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.430
He knew talent when he saw it.

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:51.170
And he knew how to make that talent a household
name.

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:54.690
Elvis finally met Parker on January 15, 1955.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:59.120
Immediately, Parker began to muscle into the
young man’s career, pushing Elvis’ other

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:00.740
manager out the picture.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:05.390
At times, this was done via ballsy moves,
like stumping up $10,000 of his own money

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:09.710
to buy Elvis out his Sun Records contract
so he could move to the bigger label RCA.

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:13.800
At others, it was done via almost sociopathic
behavior.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:17.820
Within days of James Dean’s death in a car
wreck that September, Parker was on the phone

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:23.110
to Warner Bros., insisting they just stick
Elvis in any unfinished Dean films.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:27.580
But if Elvis disapproved of these methods
- or of the man himself - he didn’t show

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:28.580
it.

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:33.520
On November 21, 1955, Elvis signed the RCA
contract Parker had negotiated.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:38.640
It netted him $5,000, plus a guarantee that
he’d play on television within 60 days.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:44.971
On January 28, 1956, Elvis sang for the first
time to a national audience on CBS’s Stage

00:11:44.971 --> 00:11:45.971
Show.

00:11:45.971 --> 00:11:48.649
At the time, Elvis was a mere 20-years old.

00:11:48.649 --> 00:12:40.510
By the time he turned 21, he would be the
biggest star on

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:48.910
the planet.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:13:12.529
(TITLE): The 
King

00:13:12.529 --> 00:13:23.560
of Controversy

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:27.360
When Elvis arrived on the national stage,
it was amid a whirlwind of controversy.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:32.110
We don’t just mean as a white guy playing
black music, although that annoyed plenty.

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:36.870
We mean something almost more concerning to
1950s prudes.

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:37.870
Elvis was sexy.

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:41.670
You know those images of Beatlemania, with
teenage girls screaming their heads off for

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.350
any Beatle who wasn’t Ringo?

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:45.600
That was Elvis in 1956.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:50.420
Something about the way he wiggled his hips
just seemed to send teenage uteruses wild.

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:53.610
And that meant sending the parents of those
teenagers into meltdown.

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:58.640
At a concert in Jacksonville, Florida, a judge
issued a pre-emptive arrest warrant against

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:03.930
Elvis for “impairing the morals of minors”
should he swivel his hips in the state.

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:08.640
In his hometown of Tupelo, a hundred National
Guardsmen surrounded the stage to stop a riot

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:09.709
from breaking out.

00:14:09.709 --> 00:14:10.709
This wasn’t music.

00:14:10.709 --> 00:14:11.890
It was something dangerous.

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:15.649
The oral equivalent of James Dean in Rebel
Without a Cause.

00:14:15.649 --> 00:14:20.640
But Elvis had the good luck to ride this cultural
wave just as the mainstream caught on.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:26.149
At the beginning of 1956, an outraged Ed Sullivan
declared he’d never have Elvis on his show.

00:14:26.149 --> 00:14:30.410
By September, Elvis was playing the Ed Sullivan
Show before an estimated 60 million people

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:34.180
- 80 percent of the viewing audience.

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:39.080
1956-Elvis may have started out dangerous
and sexy, but the mainstream was already embracing

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:40.080
him.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:43.730
Making him rich, but also leaving him about
as edgy as Dennis the Menace.

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:49.720
By January, 1957, Ed Sullivan was even telling
his viewers that Elvis “is a decent, fine

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:50.720
boy.”

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:54.329
But the real nail in his rebel coffin arrived
in December.

00:14:54.329 --> 00:14:56.680
That was the month Elvis was drafted.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:00.310
The King may have played the rebel onstage
but, when it came down to it, he was a polite

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:01.779
Southern patriot.

00:15:01.779 --> 00:15:04.279
He accepted his commission without complaint.

00:15:04.279 --> 00:15:09.360
For teens in the grip of Elvismania, this
was the cultural equivalent of Armageddon.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:13.410
Tens of thousands of letters flooded the draft
board, begging for Elvis to be exempted.

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:15.279
But the King wanted to go.

00:15:15.279 --> 00:15:18.240
On 24 March, 1958, Elvis reported for duty.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:22.500
His hair and sideburns were shorn off in front
of the cameras, and he was packed off for

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:23.500
basic training.

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:27.910
Before he went, Colonel Parker made the boy
record a ton of singles to be slowly released

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:30.860
over the next two years, to stop his star
from fading away.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:34.980
But while Parker would succeed in keeping
Elvis in the public’s mind, he wouldn’t

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:40.370
be able to maintain the sexy aura Elvis had
projected just a couple of years before.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.110
That was now gone forever.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:46.970
Over in Germany, Elvis began the newest, and
perhaps ickiest chapter of his life.

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:51.420
In September, 1959, the 24-year old star met
Priscilla Beaulieu, who you’ll know better

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:53.889
as the future Priscilla Presley.

00:15:53.889 --> 00:15:54.889
The problem?

00:15:54.889 --> 00:15:57.090
In 1959, Priscilla was only 14.

00:15:57.090 --> 00:16:00.130
And so we come to the most difficult part
of today’s video.

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:02.660
It’s true that this was a different time.

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:07.300
Jerry Lee Lewis, Chuck Berry, Charlie Chaplin
and - over the next decade and a half - Jimmy

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:11.500
Page, David Bowie, and Iggy Pop would all
have similar underage relationships.

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:16.730
On the other hand, a man in his twenties having
a relationship with a girl of 14 was as immoral

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:18.580
then as it is now.

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:20.040
And Elvis seemed to know this.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:23.540
Although he and Priscilla did many things
together, they always stopped short of sex

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:26.860
- presumably so Elvis could avoid a statuatory
rape charge.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:31.269
The worst of Elvis’s behavior is usually
attributed to his later-life drug addiction.

00:16:31.269 --> 00:16:36.490
But his seduction of an underage girl suggests
there were problems that stemmed from someplace

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:37.820
much darker.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:42.339
The King’s stint in the army finally ended
on March 2, 1960.

00:16:42.339 --> 00:16:45.940
That day, he was put on a military transport
and flown back to the States.

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:48.790
Just weeks later, he cut the Elvis is Back
album.

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:52.829
After two long years in the wilderness, the
King had finally returned.

00:16:52.829 --> 00:16:59.040
(TITLE): “What happened to good old rock
'n' roll?”

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:01.050
The Sixties were a weird time to be Elvis.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:05.179
On the one hand, this was the decade when
he became so stinking rich that he never had

00:17:05.179 --> 00:17:06.669
to worry about money again.

00:17:06.669 --> 00:17:11.449
On the other hand, it was also the decade
when things started going a little… weird.

00:17:11.449 --> 00:17:14.240
Let’s tackle the successes first.

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:19.819
Between 1960 and 1968, Elvis released some
30 movies; nearly all of them bringing him,

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:23.010
the studio, and Colonel Tom Parker a tidy
profit.

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:27.270
He also kept recording hits, although these
were rarely “hits” in the era-defining

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:29.510
way That’s All Right had been.

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:34.580
Still, Elvis and his family comfortably entered
the 1%, allowing him to finally marry the

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:38.460
no-longer-underage Priscilla in Las Vegas
in 1967.

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:41.890
Less than a year later, their daughter, Lisa
Marie Presley, was born.

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:43.660
OK, that’s the highlights.

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:46.780
Now let’s dig into the less-good stuff.

00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:51.260
Although Elvis was doing well financially,
the movies and songs bringing that money in

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.260
were… not great.

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:57.130
Artistically, watching 60s’ Elvis was like
watching Michelangelo follow up the Sistine

00:17:57.130 --> 00:18:02.150
Chapel by dedicating his life to paintings
of dogs playing poker.

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:05.700
You can admire the craft, but… really?

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:10.480
The slump in Elvis’s taste can be easily
seen in his legendary 1965 meeting with the

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:11.480
Beatles.

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:15.590
Although he and the Fab Four eventually had
a good time jamming together, John Lennon’s

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:17.010
opening remark to the King was:

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:21.330
“'Why do you do all these soft-centred ballads
for the cinema these days?

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:23.550
What happened to good old rock 'n' roll?'”

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:27.610
It’s not the only nail Lennon hit squarely
on the head that day.

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:32.020
After their jam session, Lennon told people
that Elvis had been stoned the entire time.

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:36.440
Although the King’s hefty drug use is usually
said to have started later, it seems by the

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:39.340
mid-60s he was already on that slippery slope.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:43.820
It was also during the ‘60s that Elvis likely
began to develop his eating disorder.

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:48.010
The first references come from the start of
the decade, with interviewers remarking how

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:50.390
Elvis liked to eat piles of junk food.

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:54.380
Before the ‘60s were out, the King was turning
to comfort eating more and more.

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:58.140
We’ve read people say that, when he was
feeling low, eating was the only thing that

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:00.200
could make Elvis feel normal again.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.440
Even his new marriage had its dark side.

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:05.919
For whatever weird reason, Elvis was obsessed
with virginity.

00:19:05.919 --> 00:19:08.140
Priscilla later told an interviewer that Elvis:

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:11.860
“had mentioned to me before we were married
that he had never been able to make love to

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:14.120
a woman who had a child.”

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:15.720
And that hangup remained.

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:20.130
After Lisa Marie Presley was born, Elvis never
had sex with Priscilla again.

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:23.880
Within a few years, the lack of contact would
drive Priscilla to have an affair that would

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:24.880
end their marriage.

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:28.600
Yet, despite all this, the decade ended on
something of a high.

00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:35.030
In early 1968, Tom Parker had cut a deal with
NBC to do a bland one hour special of Elvis

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:36.330
singing Christmas songs.

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:40.910
But when the project got underway, it slipped
out the Colonel’s control, and instead became

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:42.760
a music industry legend.

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:47.220
Sick of being a bland mass entertainer, Elvis
concocted something that would become known

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:49.450
as the ‘68 Comeback Special.

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:54.180
Clad in black leather, his figure once again
trim, Elvis strode onto the stage, for the

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:55.790
last time exuding cool.

00:19:55.790 --> 00:20:00.919
In a wide-ranging set, he rocked the classics,
did some unplugged acoustic, and engaged in

00:20:00.919 --> 00:20:02.310
banter with the audience.

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:06.730
By the time the curtain went down, over 42
percent of the nation had tuned in to watch,

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:08.680
and the feeling was undeniable.

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:13.030
1956 Elvis - sexy, dangerous Elvis - was back.

00:20:13.030 --> 00:20:14.650
But it was a fleeting moment.

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:18.929
A last gasp of rock n’ roll from a bygone
performer.

00:20:18.929 --> 00:20:23.539
A few months later, Elvis began his residency
at the Las Vegas International Hotel, getting

00:20:23.539 --> 00:20:27.440
paid $1m annually to entertain tourists over
their lobster dinner.

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:33.100
And so, in the big fight in Elvis’ head
between commercial success and artistic expression,

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:36.440
it was commercial success which won.

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:42.490
(TITLE): The King is Gone (but not forgotten)

00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:46.440
The last 8 years of Elvis’s life were like
watching a slow-motion clown-car crash; disturbing,

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:48.760
but also kind of darkly amusing.

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:54.190
By Christmas, 1970, Elvis was so rich and
so weird that he was able to spend $100,000

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:58.059
on 32 handguns and 10 Mercedes as a present
to himself.

00:20:58.059 --> 00:21:02.410
When Priscilla hit the roof, he stormed out
the house, got on a jet to Washington and

00:21:02.410 --> 00:21:04.040
declared that he wanted to meet the President.

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:08.720
Which is how Elvis Presley came to be photographed
shaking hands with Richard Nixon, who was

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:13.570
so grateful he gave the King a badge from
the federal Bureau of Narcotics and Dangerous

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:14.570
Drugs.

00:21:14.570 --> 00:21:19.680
By now, the once-polite Southern boy was rich
enough to do whatever he wanted, surrounded

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:23.730
by toadying yes men, and deep into a drug
habit.

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:27.660
And what he wanted to do was keep on digging
deeper.

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:32.860
In February, 1972, Elvis and Priscilla separated,
with their divorce coming through a year later.

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:36.730
With his wife and child gone, Elvis seems
to have lost the last rope tethering him to

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:37.730
planet Earth.

00:21:37.730 --> 00:21:40.890
Oh, sure, he still packed out the International
in Las Vegas.

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:44.350
He still found time to record late hits like
Suspicious Minds.

00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:48.990
But his life was by now simply one long-delayed
suicide attempt.

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:55.090
By 1975, Elvis was living as an almost total
recluse in Graceland, only waking up long

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:59.350
after the sun had set, like some junk food-addicted
vampire.

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:04.140
Speaking of junk food, what had begun as comfort
eating had by now become an ugly obsession.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:09.361
Elvis’s favorite late-life snack was Fool’s
Gold Loaf, which involves taking a whole loaf

00:22:09.361 --> 00:22:14.220
of hollowed-out bread, and cramming it full
of peanut butter, jelly, and half a kilo of

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:16.940
bacon, before deep-frying the whole thing.

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:20.500
The average male consumes around 2,500 calories
a day.

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:24.140
A single Fool’s Gold Loaf contains over
8,000.

00:22:24.140 --> 00:22:26.830
And Elvis never stopped with just a single
loaf.

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:31.169
In his last few months, Elvis started living
with 21-year old Ginger Alden, who witnessed

00:22:31.169 --> 00:22:33.990
his behavior degenerate into pure self-destruction.

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.490
He would do stuff like fire his handguns at
the TV.

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:40.230
Once, he put a bullet into the wall next to
her head when she tried to make him stop eating

00:22:40.230 --> 00:22:41.230
yoghurt.

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:45.020
Remember earlier, when we tried to imagine
what it must’ve been like to be Elvis in

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:46.020
the summer of 1954?

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:48.260
Well, let’s try again now.

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:52.130
Try to imagine what it must’ve felt like
to be trapped in this sagging body.

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:54.920
Locked away in Graceland, never seeing daylight.

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:59.960
Your soul nothing but an empty void you keep
trying to fill with food and pills.

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:04.600
Now compare those two mental images and ask
yourself: what the hell happened?

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:07.660
Finally, all the abuse got too much for Elvis’s
body.

00:23:07.660 --> 00:23:12.800
On August 16, 1977, the King died in the bathroom
of his Graceland mansion; allegedly while

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:14.120
sat on the throne.

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:15.820
He was only 42 years old.

00:23:15.820 --> 00:23:20.550
When the news broke that the King was gone,
mourners gathered in vast crowds at Graceland.

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:24.980
Although the man who’d died had been a bloated,
drug addicted mess, that wasn’t the man

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:25.980
they were there to remember.

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:30.620
They were there to remember the boy who, for
a few short years in the 1950s, had changed

00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:35.330
their lives; opened them up a new and dangerous
thing called rock n’ roll.

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:39.200
As Bruce Springsteen later said of Elvis’s
early years: “It was like he whispered his

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:42.860
dream in all our ears and then we dreamed
it.”

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:45.120
Today, the jury is still out on Elvis Presley.

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:49.130
For every person whose teenage years were
lit up by his voice, there is someone else

00:23:49.130 --> 00:23:54.660
who only ever saw him as the sweaty fat guy
in Vegas, the bygone buffoon who died while

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:55.660
taking a crap.

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:58.559
Yet there’s no doubt that Elvis was an important
figure.

00:23:58.559 --> 00:24:03.450
Here was a man who almost single-handedly
forged an entire era in pop culture.

00:24:03.450 --> 00:24:06.370
Take away Elvis and you have no Beatles.

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:07.370
No Rolling Stones.

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:08.370
No foundation for modern music.

00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:09.950
He may have done some morally dubious things.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:12.809
He may have ended his life as a parody of
his former self.

00:24:12.809 --> 00:24:17.090
But there’s no doubt that Elvis deserves
to be remembered not as a joke, but as that

00:24:17.090 --> 00:24:22.750
young man in the summer of 1954, the window
rolled down as he drives to his next gig,

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:35.670
ready to change 
the world.

